update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('cea60072-071d-439c-a650-59ec228dc961'::uuid,'535c3b6eef49177ed0a563a11dba4e98','Diagram it: `good story → praised → does well`. The fact is `~does well`, and the conclusion is `~good story`. That runs the chain backward through the contrapositive, which is valid. The stimulus negated the first condition, not the last outcome. A valid argument can''t copy a flawed pattern, so A is out.'),
('090bb381-983a-4c25-8f3d-74ddd748e1df'::uuid,'f68b850faa7e8a65240912232594e91d','Diagram it: `several stars → box office success → long run`. The second sentence has no "if", but "A box office success has a long run at theaters" is still a rule. The film features only one star, so it does not have several: `~several stars`. The conclusion is `~long run`.

That''s the stimulus exactly: chain two rules, negate the first condition, then negate the last outcome. And it fails the same way. A one-star film could still have a long run for other reasons, just as an unaccepted film''s backers could still recoup. When a Parallel Flaw stimulus is conditional, translate each answer into arrows and check three things: do the rules chain, which end does the fact touch, and what does the conclusion say about the other end?'),
('d1b9dc11-d6aa-4929-9aeb-7a967da33a3a'::uuid,'9d8d785c0bc5ad562e8afbc205b7da2f','Diagram it: `talented director → praised → does well on video`. The fact is `does well on video`, and the conclusion is `talented director`. That affirms the last outcome and concludes the first condition, a reversal. It''s flawed, but it''s the wrong flaw. The stimulus negated the first condition and concluded the outcome was negated.'),
('0b32cbfd-173d-4fdd-84f7-f521965dbb7f'::uuid,'8031a1e8cce3f29dcb92a80fc4e286ec','D looks like a chain, but it isn''t one. The first rule ends with a star being "nominated for an award"; the second starts from a star who "receives an award". Those don''t link. The fact also negates "nominated", the outcome of the first rule, not its trigger. From `~nominated` we could validly conclude `~box office success`. The stimulus negated the first trigger, so the patterns don''t match.'),
('048311b7-1be7-44d0-8681-4ddf1394aa6c'::uuid,'a0ca16f69703db637ac0be7c46a13e9e','Diagram it: `big budget → heavily promoted → released all over the country`. The fact is `big budget`, and the conclusion is that the film will be "released all over the country at once". That runs the chain forward, which is valid. The stimulus''s fact negated the first condition instead of confirming it, so E has the right chain and none of the flaw.'),
('ddedb850-2b46-46e6-8f9e-c03f4c054790'::uuid,'3ec7b29bca183d00786e3deb4177d8ca','A is tempting because it also runs through a list of criteria that someone fails. But its flaw is different. Officers need two years'' standing, committee membership, or special qualifications. Evelyn fails the first two, and the argument ignores the third route: Evelyn might have special qualifications. That''s overlooking an option, not jumping from one extreme to its opposite. The conclusion, that Evelyn "cannot be an officer," is simply not-X, not anti-X.'),
('5fea0e4e-46fc-44e6-9853-9e604ee3b872'::uuid,'2ab8d884f6e38856965edbe7baeb2a22','B lists two things a plant needs to thrive, shows this plant has neither, and offers that as the explanation for why the plant "is not as healthy as it should be." You could question that explanation, since something else might also be hurting the plant. But B never treats two opposites as the only options, so there''s no skipped middle ground and no false choice to match.'),
('655f6da9-c8e4-4ea5-acfc-a2535aca3ade'::uuid,'dbaabf18af15d974da5ed2f6d1f6ee58','C runs the same false choice in the other direction. The evidence: the book "hasn’t received even one hostile review." So it lacks the mark of the bad extreme. The conclusion: "all the critics have loved this book," the good extreme. But a critic can review a book without hostility and without love. Mixed, mild, or indifferent reviews sit in between. Not hostile does not mean loving, just as not a friend does not mean an enemy.

The move to repeat: name the two categories in the stimulus (friend, enemy), ask whether there is a middle, then find the answer that skips a middle of its own. The topic and the direction can change; the skipped middle can''t.'),
('a50fc229-f56d-4335-9b73-0ad6407ce44e'::uuid,'213d52aee72a29a1f97739dfc8d25c03','D says developing the northern border "logically implies" the other borders would be "equally acceptable," then concludes it''s "possible" one of them will be developed. Whatever you think of that, it sorts nothing into opposite categories. It moves from acceptable to possible, a modest claim, so there''s no ignored middle ground to match the stimulus.'),
('fe5473dc-f56b-44cb-a977-0bdfbfad9bae'::uuid,'7f046fc982b85e2627307aef17c82d68','E has a different flaw. The rule says that if everyone were an author, poet, or academic, society would "come crashing to a halt." Showing that most people are none of these only shows the trigger isn''t met. Concluding that society will not crash is an illegal negation: the rule never said that''s the only way to crash. There''s no false choice between two opposites.'),
('c2c428cf-f4a2-4b9a-912b-d34ef026d7d8'::uuid,'e628e0abd5650ae1f271e2c34ed2fcd6','Map it: some high school teachers (A) teach biology (B). No kindergarten teachers (C) teach biology. The conclusion is "biology is not taught by all teachers." That isn''t Some C''s are not A. It''s a claim about teachers in general, and it follows as long as there are kindergarten teachers. A valid argument with the wrong conclusion shape.'),
('1599910d-1470-46d9-aa95-bffeeeef2d0c'::uuid,'59a04b7d5c1bfaa080bc1318377279b4','B starts with Most, not Some: most school board members were once teachers. No former teacher prefers administrative work to teaching. The conclusion is "few school board members prefer administrative work to teaching." That stays about the first group, the board members, so there''s no reversal. Different quantifiers and a different shape, so it can''t match.'),
('52724ca9-4324-4289-b2f8-4d407316e52f'::uuid,'8bb19cf79baeb555858026b6010b1159','Map it: some students (A) prefer history to mathematics (B). No member of the Calculus Club (C) prefers history to mathematics. Conclusion: some members of the Calculus Club are not students, Some C''s are not A. That''s the recipe exactly.

It makes the same mistake, too. The premises prove that some students (the history fans) are not in the Calculus Club. They say nothing about whether every club member is a student, and every club member could be one. On quantifier Parallel Flaw, give letters to the repeating ideas, write the recipe, and check which group the conclusion is about. Here the whole flaw lives in that last check.'),
('7d8209de-37a6-4b17-9447-5e9d25e6fd01'::uuid,'76195b36caf796b3dfa07103c720890c','Map it: some principals (A) are harsh disciplinarians (B). No adviser to a debate team (C) is a harsh disciplinarian. Conclusion: some principals are not advisers to a debate team, Some A''s are not C. That''s the valid conclusion, the one the stimulus should have drawn. D has every ingredient but repairs the flaw, so it can''t parallel a flawed argument.'),
('7b3046cc-ddf8-42cb-9176-00d87eba3b99'::uuid,'3f3e8c153db6507fe10058d0650ecc12','Map it: the shared idea is letting students leave early. Some popular teachers (A) let their students leave early (B). No coaches (C) let students "leave before three o’clock," which only lines up if early means before three. Conclusion: some popular teachers are not coaches, Some A''s are not C. That''s the valid direction, like D, not the stimulus''s reversal.'),
('6a014f5d-f409-495b-8e0c-c1e625c32521'::uuid,'ed756a8bbe2715053055dd49982bb6ea','A''s premises fit the first half of the pattern: Bryker students excel, and they wouldn''t excel without good teachers. But the conclusion isn''t "it''s good teachers, not something else, that explains it." It''s a recommendation that "all schools should hire good teachers," which stretches one school''s situation to every school. Different conclusion, different flaw.'),
('b7c5165c-f1ad-4865-8691-d6bdf0473bcf'::uuid,'b2d2eee33d0dc7e209ebbf9c0051d25a','Line it up. Snowy days have more crashes (the Labradors are well behaved). Crashes don''t happen unless people are careless (no pet is well behaved without training). So carelessness, "not icy roads," causes the crashes when it snows (training, not genetics, explains the good behavior).

Same flaw, too. Carelessness may be required for a crash, but icy roads can still be part of the cause: a careless driver might get away with it on a dry road and crash on ice. When a conclusion says "X, not Y, is the cause" and the evidence only shows X is necessary, look for the answer that dismisses a rival factor the same way.'),
('0dcf5505-af40-44e0-bf43-7eab1e7a888d'::uuid,'5ade91123a16dbc05453de2070520c43','C is tempting because it has the same "X, not Y" conclusion. But it has no "requires" premise. It compares two groups the speaker knows (musicians dance well, mathematicians dance badly), then credits "a sense of rhythm" over "the ability to count," two ideas the premises never mention. Nothing in C plays the role of the training premise.'),
('fddaf063-2025-4f33-8227-98b9578092c3'::uuid,'ba574a3b234c0cc0bcbc0a01ace3fcb4','D goes straight from the good cooks in the speaker''s country using butter to "you must use butter" to be a good cook. There''s no necessary-condition premise and no rival cause being dismissed. Its flaw is stretching one country''s cooks into a rule for everyone. The stimulus''s flaw needs a "this, not that" cause built on a requirement.'),
('371441c9-5e10-4638-b549-52607e70978f'::uuid,'560061592c6fdb3a2c9527ce9557defc','E concludes what did not earn the A''s: no good work. But it never names a necessary condition and crowns it the cause over a rival. Its evidence is about understanding (no student who can''t solve word problems understands algebra adequately), and its flaw is treating weak understanding as proof of no good work. Different flaw.'),
('69155bf9-5b9d-48b3-a4d1-b93faafa503b'::uuid,'b348733ff5c0db590e11b9f83cb66d0a','A puts the compound on the wrong side. "A snowflake will melt if it is in a warm place and not protected" gives `warm and ~insulated → melt`, so the compound is sufficient. Then A denies part of that sufficient condition and concludes `~melt`. That''s an illegal negation: an insulated snowflake could still melt some other way. The buyer''s argument is valid, so A doesn''t match.'),
('0f36ae99-a71a-4237-a008-7a3366c50822'::uuid,'c38c136765d180e144ad1a694d935eb1','B''s rule says a stuffed animal, "in order to pass inspection, must be free of sharp edges and completely sealed." That''s `pass → sharp-free and sealed`, the same compound necessary condition as the buyer''s rule. The hippo meets one requirement (free of sharp edges) and fails the other (not completely sealed). The contrapositive, `~sharp-free or ~sealed → ~pass`, then gives `~pass`.

Same pieces, same valid move. On a parallel question with a compound condition, check three things before you care about the topic: which side of the arrow the "and" sits on, which parts the facts confirm or deny, and what the conclusion says about the other side.'),
('e151027e-6002-4d80-8bce-75125c137bac'::uuid,'25580d58c8372c540265c449724b5cfa','C''s compound is on the sufficient side: `graded and ~bumps → accessible`. Both parts are met, so `accessible` follows. Valid, but the opposite shape: the buyer''s rule lists requirements and one fails, while C lists a recipe for success and every ingredient is present.'),
('f3b8c88b-4830-42e5-a794-6f95a4981773'::uuid,'f69e1a3b3be674b6bde28beacc030d1a','D gets the compound on the right side, `accurate → meaning and meter`, which makes it the most tempting wrong answer. But both requirements are met, and the conclusion says the translation is accurate. Meeting the requirements doesn''t prove the result; that''s a reversal. The buyer denied one requirement and validly concluded no purchase, so D differs in its facts and its validity.'),
('7f4dba36-ff9f-419f-a03f-f3588e4834c4'::uuid,'de028f847bf6dfd169dc5c86871f74ed','E''s rule matches: `call meeting → quorum and two-thirds`. The facts don''t. Only quorum is confirmed, and nothing is denied. The conclusion, "unless the motion does not pass by two-thirds, this assembly may call a meeting," means that if the motion passes, the assembly may call one. That treats meeting the requirements as enough, a reversal, and it ends on a conditional.'),
('da9fcf0a-8518-4d18-8935-7dcd34f8cf35'::uuid,'d41b4c73d217febc33d608781cc2c0e5','A has one premise and a conclusion. It compares how many apartments of each kind have tubs, then jumps to percentages with no information about how many apartments of each kind exist. The stimulus needed its second premise, the total counts, to get from numbers to proportions. A is missing that piece, which also makes its conclusion unsupported.'),
('4666020b-1f36-4811-9787-5a6417d73926'::uuid,'04a39c8f54c077440bd2acb095348bbb','B has every piece in its role. More large Victorian apartments than modern apartments have tubs, so the modern group has fewer successes. There are more modern apartments than Victorian apartments, so the modern group is larger. Conclusion: a higher proportion of large Victorian apartments have tubs. Fewer hits in the bigger group, so a smaller share. B says "greater number" where the stimulus said "smaller number," but it also names the groups in the other order, so the relationship is the same.

It even copies the modifier shift: "large Victorian apartments" in the first premise and the conclusion, plain "Victorian apartments" in the middle. On a numbers-and-proportions parallel, label each group by its role (fewer successes, larger total) and check that the roles line up, not just the vocabulary.'),
('858c22e1-bb09-4f0c-bd88-bd22e9649f32'::uuid,'ac2b8809941dbcc7c88d9eb3b8e4de1f','Check the indicator. "For" marks what follows as evidence, so C''s conclusion is its first sentence: a smaller number of small modern apartments have tubs. C uses the proportion comparison as a premise to prove the number comparison. The stimulus went the other way, from numbers to a proportion. Same ingredients, reversed roles.'),
('1113f185-7505-4226-9877-9816aecdd551'::uuid,'e6f7f7ea5110b34fd015506c6b0ad3f7','The words "so" and "since" show D''s structure: the number with tubs and the percentage with tubs are the premises, and the conclusion is about how many apartments of each kind exist. The stimulus used the totals to prove a proportion; D uses a proportion to prove the totals. The proportion claim is in the wrong role.'),
('ab7439ea-1472-49d0-9e63-d697f2fe2b15'::uuid,'273bce63f26ceccdf21fd17e20fec265','E''s premises point the same direction, which breaks the logic. Modern apartments have fewer tubs, and there are also fewer modern apartments. A smaller group with fewer successes could have any share: 10 modern apartments with 5 tubs is 50%, while 100 Victorian apartments with 20 tubs is 20%. The stimulus worked because the group with fewer successes was the larger one. E''s modifier is also misplaced: "large" appears only in the middle premise.'),
('cf75fe04-f5b9-4b3a-bd51-7002547fbc78'::uuid,'8c56a10c1d2939e998f566772d224634','Diagram it: `mayor approves and council approves → gardens open this week`. The council approved. The gardens "cannot open this week." Conclusion: the mayor must not have approved. That''s `(A and B) → C`, `B`, `~C`, so `~A`: the stimulus''s recipe with the two conditions named in the other order, which doesn''t matter with "and".

It''s valid for the same reason. If the gardens can''t open, at least one approval is missing, and the council''s is in, so the mayor''s is the missing one. The move: diagram the rule, then mark each fact as confirming or denying one specific piece of it.'),
('458b51b5-1fc5-459f-8501-6b021071d55d'::uuid,'0d30b25dd004603d3513503910388847','B''s rule puts an "or" on the necessary side: `sci-fi movie → love it or hate it`. The fact denies one option (not hate), and the conclusion picks the other, and only "probably." The stimulus had an "and" on the sufficient side and denied the result to knock out a condition. Different pieces, and a hedged conclusion where the stimulus said "must."'),
('3a29a908-1a51-400a-8395-c5ec6c9c3cf0'::uuid,'85f5a5f774c31c8387d24b5dd5bd5711','C has an "or" on the sufficient side: `government or other party buys → economy improves`. The economy did not improve, so neither bought the plant. That''s a valid contrapositive, but nothing is confirmed and there''s no single missing piece to pin down. The stimulus confirmed one "and" condition and concluded the other failed.'),
('ff244581-5bf2-4b56-952e-07576b441e70'::uuid,'8122e7754ac09186c9916aac666fd83e','D''s rule runs the other way: on sale only if used or paperback, so `on sale → used or paperback`. The book is on sale, which triggers the rule, and the conclusion is an if-then: if not a paperback, then used. Valid, but it applies a rule forward to a fact. The stimulus denied a result and concluded that a condition failed.'),
('3a5ffece-90a9-4efe-adc1-49e992d63175'::uuid,'9d78da4f0722e2360e304467700b574b','E''s rule is `owes too much → higher paying job or bankruptcy`, and Doria does owe too much, so the rule is triggered forward. Then the fact is only that Doria "does not want to declare bankruptcy," which isn''t the same as not declaring it. So E shifts from wanting to doing, and its "or" result has nothing to do with breaking apart an "and" condition.'),
('98eafbb3-ae00-4a98-a8aa-bba239b3a6c6'::uuid,'3e0e34bd80fabe40606a8494574e0727','A has no dispute in it. The instructor gives a rule (proper technique means no repetitive-motion injuries), the speaker uses proper technique, and the conclusion predicts no injuries. That''s applying a rule to a case, not weighing an unbiased examiner against someone else''s claim. There''s no rival claim to be suspicious of.'),
('c26225ce-8026-4565-a9e3-4323a3205c15'::uuid,'b658103af02b05e1ab3348906dc03b46','Map the pieces. Gardner "has no vested interest in the case" (the inspector has no bias). Gardner "inspected the bones carefully" (the inspector thoroughly checked the tracks). Gardner says the bones aren''t old enough to be from dinosaurs, which contradicts Penwick''s claim (the inspector''s finding contradicts the reporter). Conclusion: "we should be skeptical of Penwick’s claim" (we should be suspicious of the reporter''s claim). Even the strength matches: doubt, not disproof.

The order is shuffled, with the careful inspection moved to the end, but every role is filled. On parallel questions, match roles and conclusion strength, not sentence order.'),
('2e4a5acf-2816-4fbc-a957-4fbc34dee1f0'::uuid,'9a1b4c16e23ae0cb43958120df90764e','C drops the piece that makes the stimulus work. The engineer was "hired by the company that maintains the bridge," so the engineer may have a stake in calling it safe, the opposite of having no bias. And there''s no rival claim: C concludes the bridge is safe instead of doubting someone else''s claim.'),
('9814d6ba-c8b4-4048-a950-bf938e7a96d0'::uuid,'992b71fb39f7007fc42c406bdab843e7','D asks us to accept a claim despite the source''s possible bias, on the general grounds that reporters won''t let bias affect them when the truth will come out. The stimulus does the reverse: it relies on a source with no bias to doubt someone else. D also has only one source, so there''s no dispute between two claims.'),
('7a7a150e-7f5a-498a-8818-26ebfaf25818'::uuid,'21419059ac497e8fbe87afaba218bcae','E discounts a claim because its source, the snowblower salesperson, is biased. That attacks the speaker instead of weighing a neutral examiner''s findings against a rival claim. The stimulus never says the reporter is biased; it doubts the reporter because a careful, unbiased inspector found otherwise. E has no expert and no examination.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;